## Example — Reference Output

### Documentation story
The canonical example for documentation stories is the **Confluence Documentation Update** story:
```
agents/requirements/output/confluence-doc-update-user-story.md
```

It demonstrates:
- Multi-API documentation story (10 APIs, 3 layers)
- Role: Integration Architect
- Endpoints: N/A → section-mapping Mapping Dictionary
- 4 ACs: Format Alignment · Flow Diagrams · Full Coverage · Review & Approval
- API Tracking Checklist table with all 10 APIs
- Security blockquote warning for Confluence access
- Performance: N/A (documentation story)
- Dependencies: 3 explicit items

### Integration story
The canonical example for integration / system-API stories is the **Product Sync** story:
```
agents/requirements/output/ngomct-3727-product-sync.md
```

It demonstrates the **AC Quality Rules** in practice:
- ACs assert HTTP status codes, response body shape, and Salesforce record field values only
- No connector names, log assertions, or internal implementation details appear in any AC
- No JSON or code examples anywhere in the Jira story — all code-level detail is in Confluence
- Each endpoint scenario has its own AC (GET lookup, POST create, PUT update are separate ACs)
- Technical Reference links to Confluence rather than reproducing SOQL or composite graph JSON
- Technical Implementation Steps are prose action bullets with no code blocks
- Standard error ACs (400, 401, 409, 502) each get their own block asserting only observable output

Use both as quality benchmarks. Every generated file should meet or exceed that level
of detail and completeness.

